/ A json value is item's, not text's (ruling 2, decision 773). A json value can hold any
/ value (a number, a bool, a list, a dict), so its parent is item, not text: a value
/ carrying Type={item, kind: json} lands as an item whose kind navigates as the "json"
/ type, and `%x% is text` is false. Written in formal so the builder's Type choice is out
/ of it. Red today — type.name reads "text" (and `is text` is true today, so %isText%
/ reads true against the expected null); only the kind.name assert is held pending Ingi.
JsonIsItemsKind
- variable.set(Name=%x%, Value="{\"a\":1}", Type={name:"item", kind:"json"})
- assert.equals(Expected="item", Actual=%x!type.name%, Message="x's type name is item")
- condition.if(Left=%x%, Operator="is", Right="text") { variable.set(Name=%isText%, Value=true) }
- assert.equals(Expected=null, Actual=%isText%, Message="x is not a text")
- assert.equals(Expected="json", Actual=%x!type.kind.name%, Message="x's kind navigates as the json type")
